## Safe prerequisites
- Preserve the original artifact hash, package/version and decompilation limitations.
- Confirm the exact researcher-owned app, current Android build and dedicated device serial.
- Use synthetic accounts, resources and canaries only; no unrelated apps or destinations.
- Start with an unrooted ordinary attacker context. ADB shell, root and authorization-changing hooks do not establish ordinary-app access.

## Controlled validation
1. Use an actual declared/resolved URI and exact component from the manifest and code. The package name is not necessarily a URI scheme, and autoVerify is not proof of successful domain verification.
2. Trace consumed path/query/extras into navigation or WebView operations and verify the effective scheme/host checks and trust boundary.
3. Send a harmless synthetic canary through a researcher-controlled browser or ordinary-app harness. Use only separately approved researcher-controlled web origins when an origin is necessary.
4. For a JavaScript-bridge candidate, identify the real bridge name, annotated methods, loaded origin and intended permissions. A bridge registration alone is not proof that untrusted JavaScript reaches a sensitive method.
5. Compare valid approved and invalid unapproved inputs on the current OS. Record only observed canary behavior; a successful launch, file error or log string does not prove token theft, UXSS or account takeover.

## Evidence and conclusion
Record the exact entrypoint, attacker-controlled input, security boundary, sensitive sink, observed impact and commands/test-app source actually used. Attach redacted evidence plus successful authorized and rejected unauthorized controls. Distinguish unsupported observations, negative results and reproduced impact. No outcome or attacker capability is asserted by this plan.

